    <statement><tabular xmlns:pi="http://pretextbook.org/2020/pretext/internal" valign="top">
<row>
<cell>Give inequalities for <m>r</m> and <m>\theta</m> which
describe the region in the figure in polar coordinates. 
The arc shown is circular, and the region extends indefinitely in the
<m>y</m>-direction.  The labeled points along the <m>x</m>-axis are
<m>x = 3</m> and <m>x = 5</m>.
(Write <alert>infinity</alert> to indicate
a boundary at infinity, and enter <alert>t</alert> for
<m>\theta</m> if necessary.)
<fillin characters="15" name="AnSwEr0001"/> <m>\le r \le</m> <fillin characters="15" name="AnSwEr0002"/>
<fillin characters="15" name="AnSwEr0003"/> <m>\le \theta \le</m> <fillin characters="15" name="AnSwEr0004"/></cell>
<cell>
<image pi:generated="webwork/images/Library__FortLewis__Calc2__8-3-Polar__HGM5-08-03-19.pg-image-1.png" width="50%"/>
<p><em>(Click on the graph for a larger version.)</em></p></cell>
</row>
</tabular>
</statement>
    <answer>
      <p>
        <m>3</m>
      </p>
    </answer>
    <answer>
      <p>
        <m>\frac{5}{\cos\mathopen{}\left(t\right)}</m>
      </p>
    </answer>
    <answer>
      <p>
        <m>0</m>
      </p>
    </answer>
    <answer>
      <p>
        <m>\frac{\pi }{2}</m>
      </p>
    </answer>
    <solution><m>r=3</m>, for <m>0\le\theta\le{\pi\over2}</m>.
The vertical line <m>x=5</m> has polar equation <m>r\cos\theta=5</m>, or
<m>r=5/\cos\theta</m>.  So the region is described by
<m>0\le\theta\le\pi/2</m> and <m>3\le r\le 5/\cos\theta</m>.



</solution>
